# TUI-DESIGN-5 §2.9 / §10 (slot R5-2): the messaging verbs under a real pty — a `request` row that PERSISTS and a
# `headsup` toast that does NOT, plus §7 row 92's secret gate on a message body. Hermetic: the peer stub
# (`JEVCODE_FAULT=peer:3`) and `--mock`; no ledger, no second process, no network, no real key.
#
# STATUS — this file does not assert the far-END rows yet, and that is a dependency, not a skip.
#   `/tell`, `/headsup`, `/request` and `/inbox` are landed registry rows with validated actions (R5-2,
#   `src/tui/commands/{registry,dispatch}.ts`) and their bodies are landed §12 strings (`src/tui/toasts.ts`), but
#   the mailbox WRITE and `Engine.deliver` are R5-1's `src/cli/session.ts` arms (§2.14) — the peer stub publishes
#   no inbox. So the marks below capture the near end: the verbs resolve, the secret gate fires before the write,
#   and the surface RULES (`MESSAGE_SURFACES`) are what the unit test pins. Committed now, with its rows.
#
# run-smoke.sh needs three rows for this scenario; R5-2 owns `test/pty/**` and lands them in W5:
#   env    : r5-message) extra_env="$extra_env JEVCODE_FAULT=peer:3";;
#   check  : r5-message) grep -q 'looks like it contains a key' "$txt" && checks="$checks r5-message:s34a" || { ok=0; checks="$checks MISSING:r5-message-s34a"; }
#   select : sel_named r5-message && run r5-message 0 24 80 chat --mock --mode jev-on
#
# What it proves, beyond "it renders":
#   - §2.9's toast rules as BEHAVIOUR: a `request` is a persistent row (it needs an answer) and a `headsup` is a
#     toast that expires — the difference is visible across two marks separated by more than TOAST_INFO_MS;
#   - §7 row 92 / §12 S34a: a secret-shaped token in a `/tell` body is gated BEFORE the write — the composer's own
#     gate sees submissions, not slash-command arguments, which is the hole this row exists to close;
#   - §2.5: a multi-word title target survives the tokeniser (`/tell "fix parse_date" …`), because the positional
#     before a `rest` argument is quote-aware;
#   - §2.2 / §12 S6: the `⇄` peer cell is in the status right zone at 80 columns and absent at 79;
#   - G-R5-9 / §7 row 61: `assertNoKeyBytes` over every frame — the gate PRINTS that a key was seen, never the key.

expect \x1b\[\?25l
expect Say hi
sleep 0.4

# 1 — §2.2 / §12 S6: the peer cell at 80 columns, from the three stub peers
mark peers-80

# 2 — §7 row 92 / §12 S34a: a `/tell` body that looks like a key is held behind the `[y]` ladder. The gate is the
#     dispatcher's `detectSecrets` over the BODY, not the composer's over a submission.
send /tell mbp the key is sk-abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789abcd
sleep 0.3
send \r
expect looks like it contains a key
sleep 0.3
mark tell-secret-gate

# 3 — `[n] edit` keeps the draft: nothing was written, and the text is still there to fix
send n
sleep 0.4
mark tell-secret-declined

# 4 — §2.5: a quoted multi-word TITLE target. The positional before the rest argument is quote-aware, so the
#     body is `ping`, not `parse_date" ping`.
send /tell "fix parse_date" ping
sleep 0.3
send \r
sleep 0.4
mark tell-quoted-title

# 5 — §2.9: a `headsup` is a TOAST. Captured immediately …
send /headsup editing src/loop/engine.ts for: make the pause exact
sleep 0.3
send \r
sleep 0.3
mark headsup-toast

# 6 — … and gone after the toast duration: a broadcast nobody must answer leaves no row behind
sleep 2.6
mark headsup-expired

# 7 — §2.9 / §12 S32: a `request` is NEVER a toast. It is a persistent row, because it needs an answer.
send /request mbp pause
sleep 0.3
send \r
sleep 0.4
mark request-row

# 8 — still there well past any toast duration: that is the whole difference between the two surfaces
sleep 2.6
mark request-row-persists

# 9 — §2.9: `/inbox` reads and writes nothing; with no mailbox it is the empty state, never a spinner (§7 row 2)
send /inbox
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 0.4
mark inbox

# 10 — §2.2 / §2.13: below PEERS_MIN_COLUMNS the whole peer segment is absent, never a truncated `⇄`
resize 24 79
sleep 0.8
mark peers-79

send /exit
sleep 0.2
send \r
eof
